# Intent: Train 2 item 8, the wake feedback cue and the wake self-test (features.md Always-on; owner report 2026-11-13 that a unit which cannot hear the wake word should find out). Cue: the tone that answers a wake word (the shipped "popup") now goes through the sound policy and the hard mute (`maipai_wake_cue`) instead of playing past them; a unit with sounds off or muted answers with the face only. Self-test: the host's `system wake_selftest` command (the host already sends it and logged "Unknown system command") counts the audio frames fed to the wake engine and the results it fetched over 1.5 s and pushes a `wake_selftest` event: result alive, no_audio (the microphone feed did not move), no_fetch (audio went in, the engine returned nothing) or skipped (why=not_armed when muted or stopped, busy while speaking, listening or ringing). A failed result is repaired once by switching the wake word off and on again (repaired=1 in the event). It proves the feed and the engine loop run; it does not prove the model hears a word, because nothing is injected (the old injected-clip test needs a 55 KB recorded clip and the engine file it patched is gone). Two atomic counters and one idle one-shot timer; no task.
# Placement: unit (the wake engine and its cue run on the robot; the host only asks and reads the event)
# Upstream: firmware/xiaozhi-esp32/main/audio/engines/afe_audio_engine.cc and main/application.cc of the xiaozhi-esp32 tree (MIT)
# Check after a flash: idle and unmuted, `system wake_selftest` answers one wake_selftest event result=alive with fed above 10 and fetched above 5; muted it answers result=skipped why=not_armed. Saying the wake word gives one popup tone when sounds are on and none when sounds are off or the unit is muted.
